## The verdict record

```json
{"schema_version": 1, "ticket": "<the ticket's id below>", "verdict": "keep | fixed | obsolete | unresolved", "confidence": "high | medium | low", "evidence": "<what the verdict rests on>", "deciding_commit": "<40-hex commit> | null", "evidence_paths": ["<repository-relative path>"], "duplicate_of": "<a ticket id> | null", "root_cause": "<one line> | null", "seam_repository": "<host/owner/name> | null"}
```

Every key is required, and no other key is read: `null` where the schema allows it.

- `evidence` is never empty: what you read that decides the verdict, naming files, commits and
  behaviour.
- `deciding_commit` is the default-branch commit that fixed (`fixed`) or obsoleted (`obsolete`) the
  ticket, as its full 40-hex id, and `null` for `keep` and `unresolved`.
- `evidence_paths` lists the repository-relative paths your verdict rests on, possibly `[]`. A
  later pass verifies the ticket again when the default branch touches one of them; with none, whenever
  the default branch moves at all.
- `duplicate_of` names another open ticket of the snapshot that has the same root cause, the
  same invariant broken, however differently it is worded; `root_cause` states that root cause
  in one line, and is `null` exactly when `duplicate_of` is. Name the duplicate whichever of the
  two you think should survive: the survivor is chosen by the order below, never by you.
- `seam_repository` is the repository owning the seam the root cause sits at, or `null`.

## The rules `groom check` holds your verdicts to

- Every ticket below has exactly one verdict record.
- A `fixed` or `obsolete` verdict's `confidence` is `medium` or `high`, never `low`, and its
  `deciding_commit` is an ancestor of that repository's fetched default-branch commit above.
- A ticket at a claimed status (the `queued`, `in-progress` or `in-review` role's) is never closed or folded
  as a duplicate, and a ticket at the `accepted` role's status (Todo) is never folded; a Todo
  ticket is closed only on a `high`-confidence `fixed` or `obsolete` verdict whose
  `deciding_commit` is verified on the default branch. Answer such a ticket as you judge it all the same:
  a closing or duplicate verdict on it is left for a person, with your evidence.
- Duplicate links are joined into clusters, which tickets closing as `fixed` or `obsolete` leave.
  Each cluster keeps one survivor: a claimed ticket first, then a Todo ticket, then one in the
  repository most of its members name as `seam_repository`, then the one with the most
  evidence, then the oldest.
- Every ticket not closed is kept, and an `unresolved` one is listed for a person.

